#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""Is every launchd service serving code from `main`, with nothing uncommitted
that it could actually execute?

WHAT THIS PROTECTS. Several launchd services read their code straight from a
`WorkingDirectory` that is a git checkout — the process reads the tree AS IT
SITS, on every request or every run, not what was last deployed. If that
checkout is on a feature branch, or carries an uncommitted change to a file the
service loads, the service is running code that is in no reviewed, merged
commit — indefinitely, until a human happens to look at the checkout rather
than the running process.

THE INCIDENT THIS IS FOR. `com.convivy.library` served code from 19 hours
before `library#45` merged (2026-08-20). Not because nothing restarted the
process — it restarts fine — but because its `WorkingDirectory` pointed at the
Library crew's own checkout, and that checkout was still on the feature branch
the PR was reviewed from. The PR merged through `gh`; the local checkout was
never switched back. **A restart-on-merge fix would have restarted the
identical stale branch — that is the distinction this check is built around,
and why it is not `convivy-orient`'s "restart on merge" work in a different
hat.**

WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE CHECK, NOT A FOLD-IN. The identical defect is already
written down for THIS repo — `agent-platform`'s `bin/`/`lib/` are symlinks
into the working tree (ARCH-396), so a feature-branch checkout makes un-merged
code live to cron — and it still caught nobody when the Library hit the same
shape. A note in one repo does not travel; a scheduled check does. It is
ALSO deliberately not folded into `check-primary-checkouts`: that script
checks branch only, against a hand-DECLARED list of repos somebody has
classified as live-deployed, and its own docstring says why dirty-tree
checking is a different question ("would make one alert mean two things").
This script instead DISCOVERS every checkout a launchd plist actually serves
from, and checks both branch and dirty state — a genuinely different sweep,
not a wider version of that one. Folding this into an existing check that
already promises something narrower is how a "verified" claim quietly stops
being true; `agentctl kb sync` reporting "verified" every cycle while a
corrupt FTS index sat under it is the same failure shape, found the same day
this check was commissioned.

THE NOISE PROBLEM, AND WHY THIS DOES NOT JUST REPORT "dirty". A survey run by
hand on 2026-08-20 found `convivy-orient` serving 8 of its jobs from a
checkout carrying 7 uncommitted files — all `.claude/` individuation (a
`.materialized` marker, agent `.md` files, a skill doc) plus one stray
untracked image, none of it served. Flagging that every run trains the reader
to stop looking at the output within a week, which is worse than not checking
at all — a check nobody reads is a false "verified" with extra steps. So a
dirty file only counts as a finding when BOTH:

  - it is not under `.claude/` (Claude Code's own session/individuation
    state, never part of what any service here executes, in any repo), and
  - it is TRACKED and changed (`git status --porcelain` reports anything other
    than `??`) — an untracked file is something nobody committed yet, which is
    a different situation from a committed file whose content on disk has
    silently drifted from the commit a review approved. `com.convivy.cfo-review`
    serving from a checkout with untracked `agent-runs/`/`hook-log/`/`webapp/`
    directories alongside four MODIFIED tracked files (`schema.sql` and three
    scripts) is exactly this split: the modified files are the finding, the
    untracked directories are operational data this rule correctly ignores.

BLACK-BOX CREWS. CFO is a black-box crew (`platform/decisions/0043`) — this
check must never read a served file's CONTENT, only THAT it changed. Every
signal here comes from `git branch --show-current`/`git status --porcelain`:
a branch name, a path, a one-letter status code. Nothing here runs `git
diff`, `git show`, `git log -p`, or opens a file. That is not a carve-out
for CFO specifically — it is the design, so the same check is safe to run
against any black-box crew added later without anyone having to remember to
exempt it.

CONTENT SAFETY IS NOT THE WHOLE CONSTRAINT, and this file's first draft
missed the other half (security-reviewer, PR #423, run-0d72a1e3): a
finding's own EXISTENCE in the shared, multi-reader signal store is itself a
leak, independent of how little it says. Logging "CFO is on branch `x` with
these changed files" is exactly what ADR-0043 forbids — "a black box crew
files no issues and logs no signals" — even though the finding never quotes
a line of CFO's own code. `main()` checks `_is_private_checkout` once per
checkout, via the SAME registry lookup `session_state.py`/`tmux_transcript.py`
already use (`private_crew.private_home_for`, not a second implementation
that could drift), and a private crew's findings print to this process's own
local log and go no further — still counted, still visible to whoever reads
this machine directly, never written to `signal_client`.

WHAT COUNTS AS "SERVING". Every `~/Library/LaunchAgents/*.plist` whose
`WorkingDirectory` is a git checkout (`.git` present) under `--convivy-root`.
Deliberately not narrowed to KeepAlive/daemon jobs — a periodic job (a backfill,
a poller) reading a stale branch on every firing is the same defect, just
firing on a schedule instead of continuously. Deliberately scoped to
`--convivy-root` (default: the machine's resolved root) rather than "any WorkingDirectory that
happens to be a git repo" — Homebrew's own installation at `/opt/homebrew` is
git-managed and would otherwise show up as a false positive on a branch this
check has no opinion about.

MULTIPLE SERVICES, ONE CHECKOUT. `convivy-orient` alone backs 8 plists from one
checkout. Findings are grouped by checkout (branch/dirty state can only be
asked once per directory), and the alert names every service label that reads
it, so whoever responds knows the full blast radius rather than one instance
of it.

DELIBERATELY NOT SELF-HEALING, for the same reason `check-primary-checkouts`
is not: switching a checkout back under whoever is working in it could strand
uncommitted work, and the correct output is a loud, durable alert naming what
to run, not an unattended `git switch`.

SAFETY. Read-only. Runs `git branch --show-current` and `git status
--porcelain`, and reads plist files. Never checks out, fetches, resets, or
writes to any repo; never reads the contents of any file the check reports on.

BEHAVIOUR. A pass is silent. A finding logs an alert signal with dedup_key scoped to
the UTC day, so a finding logs once a day while it persists, and again after it clears
and returns on a later day. A finding that clears and returns within the same UTC day
logs nothing after that day's first signal, unless it returns as a different kind: the key
carries the finding kind, so a same-day return of another kind logs again. The unparseable-plist
finding is the exception: its key takes the LOCAL day from `_today()`, and it carries the count
of unparseable plists,
so a same-day return with a different count logs again. It never creates a work item
(platform/decisions/0073).

A HOST THAT CANNOT FORK IS NOT A FINDING AGAINST ANY REPOSITORY. On 2026-10-12
the operator account hit its per-process limit for 13 minutes and every
`git`/`plutil` call failed with EAGAIN; this check read that as an
`unreadable` finding against each repository it happened to be checking,
and a person had to close those findings by hand. `HostForkExhausted` makes
that condition abandon the whole pass instead — no finding of any kind is
posted for that pass (every finding this check could raise is gathered and
posted only once the pass completes), one stderr line, exit 0, and the next
900s launchd tick retries. Four consecutive skipped passes (one hour at
that interval) instead files ONE host-level finding through the same
`_post_signal` path, day-scoped like every other finding here, so a host
that stays broken is still visible — and names every NON-PRIVATE serving
checkout the last pass that actually completed examined, with the time of
that pass, so a human can see what has gone unchecked rather than just that
something has (security-reviewer, PR #871 round 2: a skip hides every
checkout's state, not only the one whose call happened to hit EAGAIN). A
private crew's checkout is filtered out of that listing, and out of the
state file it is built from, by the same `_is_private_checkout` gate every
other finding in this script already answers to before any of it reaches
the shared store (security-reviewer, PR #871 round 3).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import errno
import json
import os
import pathlib
import plistlib
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
from typing import Any
sys.path.insert(0, str(pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))
import builder_root as BR  # noqa: E402

CONVIVY_ROOT = BR.root()
LAUNCH_AGENTS = pathlib.Path.home() / "Library" / "LaunchAgents"
RUNS_ROOT = BR.runs_root()

# `plutil` only exists on macOS, which is the ONLY platform this ever runs on
# in production — it reads `~/Library/LaunchAgents`, which does not exist
# anywhere else. Resolved once at import so a missing binary is one check, not
# a `subprocess.run` OSError caught on every plist. `None` here (this repo's
# CI runs its `python` job on `convivy-linux`) routes `_plist_fields` to the
# `plistlib` fallback below, which exists ONLY to keep that job's test suite
# honest on Linux — see `_plist_fields`'s docstring for why `plutil` is
# preferred whenever it is available.
_PLUTIL = shutil.which("plutil")

# Track how many report attempts failed. Reset each run. Distinguishes
# "findings logged OK" (exit 2) from "findings but reporting failed" (exit 3) —
# same split `check-primary-checkouts` makes, for the same reason: a logging
# failure must not read on stderr identically to a clean sweep.
_report_failures: int = 0


class HostForkExhausted(Exception):
    """The HOST could not start a new process at all (EAGAIN on fork), not a
    defect in whichever repository happened to be checked when it hit.

    THE INCIDENT THIS IS FOR. On 2026-10-12, between 18:15 and 18:28 UTC, the
    operator account hit its per-user process limit and every `git`/`plutil`
    call this script makes failed with `EAGAIN` (errno 35). This script read
    that the same way it reads a genuinely broken checkout — an `unreadable`
    finding against each repository it happened to be checking — and the
    selector crew had to close those findings by hand. Raised instead of
    returned, so one subprocess call that cannot even start aborts the whole
    pass (see `main`'s fork-exhaustion handling) rather than being
    misattributed to a repository that was never the problem.
    """


def _is_fork_exhaustion(exc: OSError) -> bool:
    """Is `exc` the host refusing to fork at all, rather than anything about
    the one `git`/`plutil` call that raised it?

    `BlockingIOError` is `subprocess`'s own exception for `EAGAIN`/`EWOULDBLOCK`
    on the fork+exec path; the bare `errno.EAGAIN` check catches the same
    condition on a code path that raises a plain `OSError` instead. Checked
    once here so every call site below asks the identical question."""
    return isinstance(exc, BlockingIOError) or getattr(exc, "errno", None) == errno.EAGAIN


# ONE HOUR, NOT THE 15-MINUTE TICK ITSELF (this check's own StartInterval).
# A single transient EAGAIN must not escalate by itself — the next launchd
# tick retries for free, and this script already exists to avoid training its
# reader to ignore noise it can self-clear (see the module docstring's THE
# NOISE PROBLEM). Four consecutive skipped ticks, at this check's 900s
# interval, is one hour — the point past which "the host can't fork" has
# stopped looking transient and become a host-level incident worth a human
# looking at, rather than something the next tick will quietly fix.
FORK_SKIP_FINDING_THRESHOLD = 4

FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH = RUNS_ROOT / "check-serving-checkouts" / "fork-skip-state.json"


def _read_state() -> dict[str, Any]:
    """The state file's contents as a dict. `{}` on no file, a corrupt file,
    or a value that is not a JSON object — a state this check cannot trust is
    not a reason to crash, only to read as though nothing has ever been
    recorded."""
    if not FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH.exists():
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads(FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH.read_text())
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, ValueError, OSError):
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def _read_consecutive_skips() -> int:
    """How many passes in a row ended in `HostForkExhausted`. 0 on no file, a
    corrupt file, or a value that is not a plain count — a state this check
    cannot trust is not a reason to under-count a real outage either."""
    try:
        return max(0, int(_read_state().get("consecutive_skips", 0)))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return 0


def _read_last_checked() -> tuple[list[str] | None, str | None]:
    """(checked, checked_at) recorded by the last pass that completed without
    raising `HostForkExhausted` — the one case a skipped pass cannot speak
    for itself, since it never reached any checkout at all. `(None, None)`
    when no pass has ever completed (no file, or a file predating this
    field)."""
    state = _read_state()
    checked, checked_at = state.get("checked"), state.get("checked_at")
    if not isinstance(checked, list) or not isinstance(checked_at, str):
        return None, None
    return checked, checked_at


def _write_state(*, consecutive_skips: int, checked: list[str] | None,
                 checked_at: str | None) -> None:
    """Atomic write — a sibling temp file then `os.replace`, the same shape
    `agent-cost-report`'s `_atomic_write` uses, so a reader never sees a
    truncated file mid-write. `checked`/`checked_at` are omitted from the
    payload when the caller has nothing to carry forward — a skip's own write
    (see `_skip_pass_for_fork_exhaustion`) passes through whatever a prior
    completed pass already recorded rather than erasing it, since a skipped
    pass is not itself a completed pass and has nothing new to report about
    which checkouts exist."""
    FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    payload: dict[str, Any] = {"consecutive_skips": consecutive_skips}
    if checked is not None:
        payload["checked"] = checked
    if checked_at is not None:
        payload["checked_at"] = checked_at
    tmp = FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH.with_name(f".{FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH.name}.tmp.{os.getpid()}")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(payload))
    os.replace(tmp, FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH)

# Two candidates, same order bin/knowledge-watcher uses: this checkout's own
# lib first (so this script and the library it was written against move
# together), the installed farm second (a copied deployment). Neither existing
# is not an error: the import then falls through to PYTHONPATH, which is how a
# test reaches this checkout's own lib rather than whatever is installed on the
# machine.
_LIB_CANDIDATES = (
    pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform",
    pathlib.Path.home() / ".local" / "lib" / "agent-platform",
)


def _ensure_lib_on_path() -> None:
    """Put the platform lib on sys.path exactly once, gated on a known module.

    A wrong guess finds nothing (and falls through to PYTHONPATH) instead of
    importing a stale copy.
    """
    for libdir in _LIB_CANDIDATES:
        if (libdir / "private_crew.py").is_file():
            entry = str(libdir)
            if entry not in sys.path:
                sys.path.insert(0, entry)
            return


def _is_private_checkout(repo_dir: pathlib.Path) -> bool:
    """Is this checkout a black-box crew's (`platform/decisions/0043`)?

    THE BUG THIS CLOSES (security-reviewer, PR #423, run-0d72a1e3). This
    check's findings used to be written to the shared, multi-reader store for
    every checkout, CFO included — a signal there names CFO's branch and
    changed file paths, which is exactly what
    the ADR says must never happen: "A black box crew files no issues and
    logs no signals... so the queue and the issue-derived views will not see
    it, and should not." Never reading file CONTENT (this script's other,
    already-correct constraint) does not cover this — a finding's own
    EXISTENCE in a shared store is itself the leak, independent of what it
    says. `main()` calls this once per checkout before ever calling
    `_post_signal` for it. Returns `True` — skip logging — both when the
    checkout genuinely IS private and when that cannot be determined at all
    (the import itself failed, or `private_crew.private_home_for` returned
    its own `UNKNOWN`): fails CLOSED, matching the WRITER's own direction in
    `private_crew`'s module docstring (this function is about to decide
    whether to WRITE something to a shared store, so "cannot tell" must not
    be read as "go ahead").

    Delegates to `private_crew.private_home_for`, the SAME registry lookup
    and inode-based comparison `session_state.py`/`tmux_transcript.py`
    already use — not a second implementation of "which crew owns this
    directory" that could drift from theirs. Reusing it here also closes the
    case-sensitivity trap a naive fix would walk straight into: CFO's
    checkout directory is capital-`CFO` on disk while the registry's slug is
    lower-case `cfo`, so a bare string comparison against a slug set would
    silently never match; `private_home_for` compares real paths via
    `os.path.samefile`, not names.
    """
    try:
        _ensure_lib_on_path()
        import private_crew
    except Exception:
        return True  # cannot import the check -> cannot rule out private
    home = private_crew.private_home_for(str(repo_dir))
    return home is not None  # a real path or private_crew.UNKNOWN are both non-None


def _post_signal(product: str, title: str, body: str, dedup_key: str,
                 *, dry_run: bool = False, severity: str = "high") -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Log an alert signal for a finding. Best-effort, never raises. A signal,
    never a work item. The title leads the body, since a signal has no title field."""
    global _report_failures
    if dry_run:
        return {"status": "dry-run"}
    try:
        _ensure_lib_on_path()
        import signal_client  # noqa: E402
        status = signal_client.post_alert(
            "serving-checkout", signal_client.sanitise_body(f"{title}\n\n{body}"),
            product=product, dedup_key=dedup_key, meta={"severity": severity})
        return {"status": status}
    except Exception as exc:
        _report_failures += 1
        print(f"[REPORT-ERROR] {product}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return {"status": f"error:{type(exc).__name__}"}


def _plist_fields(path: pathlib.Path) -> tuple[dict[str, str], str]:
    """(fields, error) for one plist's `Label`/`WorkingDirectory`.

    `error` is set ONLY when the FILE ITSELF could not be parsed — genuinely
    different from a plist that parses fine and simply does not declare one
    of these keys, which is `fields` missing that key, not an error. A launchd
    job with no `WorkingDirectory` is not a defect this check has an opinion
    about; a plist this check could not even read is a gap in its own
    coverage and must be reported as one rather than silently skipped —
    reported live on 2026-08-21 as a survey-invalidating gap: a hand count run
    with `plistlib` alone silently dropped 16 of 29 real launchd-referenced
    checkouts on this exact `--`-in-comment shape, read as "13, so the scope
    is small" rather than as "16 unreadable."

    PREFERS `plutil -lint` + `-extract ... raw`, one call per key, over
    `plistlib` — found by running against this machine's real
    `~/Library/LaunchAgents/`, not synthetic fixtures: `plistlib`'s strict XML
    parser rejects a `--` sequence inside a `<!-- comment -->`, which several
    real plists here carry in their own header comments (documentation, not
    the directive). macOS's own `plutil` accepts them without complaint, and
    launchd itself clearly does too, since it runs these jobs.
    `com.selector-backend.api` is the ONLY plist naming that checkout, so
    `plistlib` alone would make an entire real, currently-running service
    invisible to this check, not merely undercounted.

    FALLS BACK TO `plistlib` only when `plutil` itself is not on this machine
    at all (non-macOS — this repo's CI runs its `python` job on
    `convivy-linux`, where `~/Library/LaunchAgents` does not exist either).
    That path exists to keep the test suite exercising real parsing logic on
    Linux, not because this script is meant to tolerate malformed plists
    there — it never runs there in production.
    """
    if _PLUTIL:
        try:
            lint = subprocess.run([_PLUTIL, "-lint", str(path)],
                                  capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
            if isinstance(exc, OSError) and _is_fork_exhaustion(exc):
                raise HostForkExhausted(str(exc)) from exc
            return {}, f"could not run plutil -lint: {exc}"
        if lint.returncode != 0:
            return {}, (lint.stderr.strip() or lint.stdout.strip()
                        or f"plutil -lint exited {lint.returncode}")
        fields: dict[str, str] = {}
        for key in ("Label", "WorkingDirectory"):
            try:
                proc = subprocess.run([_PLUTIL, "-extract", key, "raw", str(path)],
                                      capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
            except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
                if isinstance(exc, OSError) and _is_fork_exhaustion(exc):
                    raise HostForkExhausted(str(exc)) from exc
                continue
            if proc.returncode == 0:
                val = proc.stdout.strip()
                if val:
                    fields[key] = val
        return fields, ""
    try:
        with path.open("rb") as fh:
            data = plistlib.load(fh)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — plistlib's XML backend can raise
        # xml.parsers.expat.ExpatError, which is not a plistlib exception; any
        # parse failure here is the same "could not read this file" answer.
        return {}, f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}"
    fields = {k: data[k] for k in ("Label", "WorkingDirectory")
             if isinstance(data.get(k), str) and data.get(k)}
    return fields, ""


def serving_checkouts(
    launch_agents: pathlib.Path, convivy_root: pathlib.Path,
) -> tuple[dict[pathlib.Path, list[str]], dict[pathlib.Path, list[str]], list[str]]:
    """(checkouts, excluded, unparseable_plist_names).

    `checkouts` maps every git checkout UNDER `convivy_root` some launchd
    plist's `WorkingDirectory` names to the sorted labels of the services
    that read it — this is what `main()` actually checks branch/dirty state
    for. `excluded` is the same shape, for a real git checkout a plist names
    that sits OUTSIDE `convivy_root` — reported rather than silently dropped
    (steward, 2026-08-21): scoping to `convivy_root` is what correctly keeps
    Homebrew's own git-managed `/opt/homebrew` out, but the identical rule
    also drops any future checkout placed outside the machine's root for a reason
    nobody records — exactly the shape of the unparseable-plist bug this
    file already fixed once, just by design instead of by accident. Silently
    excluding is not wrong; being unable to tell "excluded" from "never
    existed" is. `unparseable_plist_names` names every plist `_plist_fields`
    could not read AT ALL — reported, not silently skipped, for the same
    reason (see `_plist_fields`).

    A plist that parses fine but declares no `WorkingDirectory`, or whose
    `WorkingDirectory` is not itself a git checkout, is correctly absent
    from all three — that is not a gap, it is "not a serving checkout at
    all," a different fact from "a checkout this scope excludes."
    """
    found: dict[pathlib.Path, list[str]] = {}
    excluded: dict[pathlib.Path, list[str]] = {}
    unparseable: list[str] = []
    try:
        plists = sorted(launch_agents.glob("*.plist"))
    except OSError:
        return found, excluded, unparseable
    convivy_root = convivy_root.resolve()
    for p in plists:
        fields, error = _plist_fields(p)
        if error:
            unparseable.append(p.name)
            continue
        label, wd = fields.get("Label"), fields.get("WorkingDirectory")
        if not label or not wd:
            continue
        wd_path = pathlib.Path(wd)
        try:
            wd_resolved = wd_path.resolve()
        except OSError:
            continue
        if not (wd_resolved / ".git").exists():
            continue
        # A real checkout. In scope only if it sits under convivy_root —
        # otherwise it is named in `excluded` rather than dropped.
        try:
            wd_resolved.relative_to(convivy_root)
        except ValueError:
            excluded.setdefault(wd_resolved, []).append(label)
            continue
        found.setdefault(wd_resolved, []).append(label)
    for labels in found.values():
        labels.sort()
    for labels in excluded.values():
        labels.sort()
    return found, excluded, sorted(unparseable)


def current_branch(repo_dir: pathlib.Path) -> tuple[str | None, str]:
    """(branch, error). branch is None on a detached HEAD or an unreadable repo.

    A detached HEAD is a finding too, and named separately rather than folded
    into "off main": it points the service at an arbitrary commit with the
    same effect as a named feature branch, while `git status` shows nothing
    that reads as a problem at a glance.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(
            ["git", "-C", str(repo_dir), "symbolic-ref", "--short", "-q", "HEAD"],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        if _is_fork_exhaustion(exc):
            raise HostForkExhausted(str(exc)) from exc
        return None, f"could not read HEAD: {exc}"
    try:
        out, err = proc.communicate(timeout=30)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        # A stalled filesystem must not leave a hung `git` behind every time
        # this runs — `subprocess.run`'s timeout raises without reaping the
        # child (the same fix `check-primary-checkouts` applies, security
        # review #354).
        proc.kill()
        proc.communicate()
        return None, "timed out reading HEAD (30s)"
    if out.strip():
        return out.strip(), ""
    if proc.returncode == 1 and not err.strip():
        return None, "detached HEAD"
    detail = err.strip() or f"git exited {proc.returncode} with no output"
    return None, f"HEAD is unreadable — not a branch and not a detached HEAD: {detail}"


def servable_dirty_paths(repo_dir: pathlib.Path) -> tuple[list[str], str]:
    """(paths, error). `paths` are the tracked, changed files outside `.claude/`
    — never file contents, only the porcelain status lines' path column.

    `git status --porcelain=v1 --no-renames` rather than the default: renames
    print as `old -> new` on one line, which this would otherwise misparse as
    a single "path" containing a literal arrow. `--no-renames` reports a rename
    as a plain delete-plus-add instead, two ordinary lines this already knows
    how to read.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(repo_dir), "status", "--porcelain=v1", "--no-renames"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        if isinstance(exc, OSError) and _is_fork_exhaustion(exc):
            raise HostForkExhausted(str(exc)) from exc
        return [], f"could not read status: {exc}"
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return [], f"git status exited {proc.returncode}: {proc.stderr.strip()}"
    servable: list[str] = []
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        if len(line) < 4:
            continue
        status, path = line[:2], line[3:]
        if status == "??":
            continue  # untracked — not yet in any commit either way
        if path.startswith(".claude/") or path == ".claude":
            continue  # Claude Code's own individuation state, never served
        servable.append(path)
    return sorted(servable), ""


def _utc_today() -> str:
    """Today's UTC date, ISO format: the day scope of a finding's dedup_key.

    A named seam so a test can pin the day. main() reads it once per run and
    passes that one value to every finding key the run writes."""
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).date().isoformat()


def _utc_now_iso() -> str:
    """Now, UTC, seconds precision, ISO-8601 — the time recorded alongside
    the last completed pass's checked-checkouts list, so the host-level
    fork-exhaustion finding can say how stale that record is. A named seam,
    same reason `_utc_today` is one: a test can pin it."""
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")


def _today() -> str:
    """Today's date, ISO format — a named seam rather than an inline
    `datetime.date.today()` call, so a test can pin that the unparseable-plist
    `dedup_key` actually varies by date (the same reason `session_state.py`'s
    `_utc_today` exists as a seam of its own).

    LOCAL TIME, DELIBERATELY, unlike session_state.py's `_utc_today`, which this is modelled on
    (security-reviewer, PR #424 round 2). One launchd job on one host writes
    this key, so there is no second writer whose UTC day could disagree with
    this one's local day, and a local day matches the run-dir convention this
    repo already uses (ARCH-156). Do NOT copy this seam anywhere with more
    than one writing host without switching it to UTC: `session_state.py`
    carries a real prior incident where a local-vs-UTC day-key mix-up
    produced up-to-a-day-stale reads, silently."""
    return datetime.date.today().isoformat()


def check(repo_dir: pathlib.Path) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
    """Every independent finding for one checkout, as `{kind, reason, paths}`.

    A LIST, NOT ONE (ok, reason) TUPLE, deliberately — branch state and
    dirty-tree state are two conditions that start and resolve on their own
    schedules. A checkout can be off `main` AND carry a dirty served file at
    the same time, and collapsing that into one reason string would let
    fixing either one silently swallow the other from the alert. Each
    dict's `kind` becomes part of the signal's `dedup_key` in `main()`, so the
    two stay independently tracked even when they co-occur.
    """
    findings: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
    branch, err = current_branch(repo_dir)
    dirty, dirty_err = servable_dirty_paths(repo_dir)
    if dirty_err:
        # A status read that failed is itself a finding — silence here is
        # exactly the false "clean" this check exists to prevent.
        findings.append({"kind": "unreadable",
                         "reason": f"could not be checked: {dirty_err}", "paths": []})
        return findings
    if branch != "main":
        where = f"on '{branch}'" if branch else err
        findings.append({"kind": "branch", "reason": where, "paths": []})
    if dirty:
        findings.append({"kind": "dirty",
                         "reason": "has uncommitted changes to served files",
                         "paths": dirty})
    return findings


def _skip_pass_for_fork_exhaustion(exc: HostForkExhausted, *, day: str, dry_run: bool) -> int:
    """Record one skipped pass and exit 0 — the launchd job's own 900s
    `StartInterval` is the retry, and a pass that could not even start a
    `git`/`plutil` call has nothing to report about any repository.

    Past `FORK_SKIP_FINDING_THRESHOLD` consecutive skips, also files ONE
    host-level finding through `_post_signal` — the same path every other
    finding in this script takes, with its own day-scoped dedup_key so it
    logs once a day while the host keeps failing to fork, exactly like the
    unparseable-plist finding above (see the module docstring's BEHAVIOUR).
    That is deliberately a finding about the HOST, never about a repository:
    this is the fix for the 2026-10-12 incident where the opposite happened.

    NAMES WHAT WENT UNCHECKED (security-reviewer, PR #871 round 2). A skip
    hides every serving checkout's branch/dirty state for that pass, not
    only the one whose `git`/`plutil` call happened to raise `EAGAIN` — a
    writer with access to just one checkout's `.git/config` or hooks, or any
    unrelated process sharing the operator account's process ceiling, can
    trigger it. The old per-repository path at least named each repository
    it could not check; this finding must too, so it names every checkout
    `_read_last_checked` recorded from the LAST PASS THAT ACTUALLY
    COMPLETED, with the time of that pass — or says plainly that no pass has
    ever completed, when there is no such record yet.

    NEVER A PRIVATE CHECKOUT (security-reviewer, PR #871 round 3). `checked`
    is already filtered to exclude any checkout `_is_private_checkout`
    answers `True` for before `main` ever writes it to
    `FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH` (see the comment where `checked` is built) — this
    function only reads back and renders what `main` already filtered, so
    there is nothing left to filter here and nothing to forget. If every
    checkout the last completed pass found was private, this correctly
    collapses to the SAME "found no serving checkouts at all" message a
    pass with genuinely nothing to check would give — a private crew's
    presence is never disclosed by itself, the same rule this script
    already applies to its per-repository findings.
    """
    print(f"[SKIP] check skipped: host resource exhaustion (EAGAIN on fork): {exc}",
          file=sys.stderr)
    skips = _read_consecutive_skips() + 1
    checked, checked_at = _read_last_checked()
    _write_state(consecutive_skips=skips, checked=checked, checked_at=checked_at)
    if skips >= FORK_SKIP_FINDING_THRESHOLD:
        if checked is None:
            unchecked = ("No pass has ever completed, so there is no record of which "
                        "serving checkouts this host has examined.")
        elif checked:
            listing = "\n".join(f"  - {c}" for c in checked)
            unchecked = (f"Unchecked since the last completed pass, at {checked_at} UTC:\n\n"
                        f"{listing}")
        else:
            unchecked = (f"The last completed pass, at {checked_at} UTC, found no serving "
                        f"checkouts at all.")
        title = "[serving-checkout] host cannot fork a new process"
        body = (
            f"**This host has failed to start a subprocess for {skips} consecutive "
            f"check(s) in a row** (`EAGAIN`/`BlockingIOError`), roughly a "
            f"`StartInterval` tick apart — the operator account is at its process "
            f"limit, not any repository this check serves.\n\n"
            f"{unchecked}\n\n"
            f"On 2026-10-12 this exact condition was misreported as a finding "
            f"against every repository this check happened to be checking instead "
            f"of against the host; this is the host-level alert that replaced that.\n\n"
            f"Fix: find and close whatever is holding processes open on this "
            f"machine. This check resumes reporting on its own once a pass completes."
        )
        res = _post_signal("agent-platform", title, body,
                           dedup_key=f"serving-checkout:host-fork-exhaustion:{day}",
                           dry_run=dry_run)
        print(f"[FINDING] host cannot fork ({skips} consecutive skips) → "
              f"{res.get('status', '?')}", file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="check-serving-checkouts", description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--verbose", "-v", action="store_true",
                        help="print per-checkout details even when passing")
    parser.add_argument("--convivy-root", default=str(CONVIVY_ROOT),
                        help="override the machine's root (for testing)")
    parser.add_argument("--launch-agents", default=str(LAUNCH_AGENTS),
                        help="override ~/Library/LaunchAgents (for testing)")
    parser.add_argument("--no-report", action="store_true",
                        help="detect findings but do not log signals (dry run)")
    opts = parser.parse_args(argv)

    global _report_failures
    _report_failures = 0

    convivy_root = pathlib.Path(opts.convivy_root)
    launch_agents = pathlib.Path(opts.launch_agents)
    day = _utc_today()

    try:
        checkouts, excluded, unparseable = serving_checkouts(launch_agents, convivy_root)
    except HostForkExhausted as exc:
        return _skip_pass_for_fork_exhaustion(exc, day=day, dry_run=opts.no_report)
    findings = 0

    if excluded:
        # A line in the output, not a finding and not a signal (steward,
        # 2026-08-21) — scoping to convivy_root is a deliberate design
        # choice, not a defect, but a silent exclusion is indistinguishable
        # from a checkout this check never learned about at all. Printed
        # unconditionally (not gated on --verbose) so the scope of a run is
        # legible from its log without reading this file's source.
        excluded_desc = "; ".join(
            f"{repo.name} ({', '.join(labels)})" for repo, labels in sorted(excluded.items()))
        print(f"[EXCLUDED] outside {convivy_root}, not checked: {excluded_desc}",
              file=sys.stderr)

    # GATHERED, NOT POSTED, UNTIL THE WHOLE PASS COMPLETES — the unparseable-
    # plist finding below included. A pass that finds a real problem (that
    # finding, or a per-repository one) and then hits `HostForkExhausted` on
    # a checkout later in `sorted(checkouts)` must not have already posted
    # anything — that leaves the run's own exit status and what is posted
    # (both decided below, only once the loop finishes without raising)
    # silently disagreeing with what the shared store holds. An abandoned
    # pass posts nothing at all; the same ground is checked again, and
    # posted then, on the next completed pass (reviewer finding, PR #871
    # round 1; the unparseable-plist finding was still posted eagerly here,
    # ahead of this boundary, until reviewer finding, PR #871 round 2).
    pending: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
    try:
        for repo_dir in sorted(checkouts):
            labels = checkouts[repo_dir]
            repo_findings = check(repo_dir)
            if not repo_findings:
                if opts.verbose:
                    print(f"[OK] {repo_dir}: on main, clean ({len(labels)} service(s))")
                continue
            services = ", ".join(labels)
            # CFO (and any future black-box crew) never reaches _post_signal — a
            # finding's own EXISTENCE in the shared signal store is the leak
            # ADR-0043 forbids, independent of the fact that this check never
            # reads file content (security-reviewer, PR #423, run-0d72a1e3). The
            # finding still counts (a real problem on a private checkout is
            # still a real problem) and still prints locally — this machine's
            # own log, not a shared multi-reader store — just never logs.
            private = _is_private_checkout(repo_dir)
            for f in repo_findings:
                pending.append({"repo_dir": repo_dir, "services": services,
                                "finding": f, "private": private})
    except HostForkExhausted as exc:
        return _skip_pass_for_fork_exhaustion(exc, day=day, dry_run=opts.no_report)

    # Only now — the per-repository loop reached the end without the host
    # ever refusing to fork — is it safe to post the unparseable-plist
    # finding (see the GATHERED comment above) and to record what this pass
    # examined.
    if unparseable:
        # Reported, not skipped — see `serving_checkouts`'s docstring. This
        # is a gap in this check's OWN coverage, not a claim about the
        # services those plists define, so it is logged once as its own
        # finding rather than attributed to any checkout.
        #
        # THE SHARED SIGNAL NEVER NAMES A FILENAME (security-reviewer, PR #423
        # round 2, non-blocking — fixed as the immediate follow-up rather
        # than left as a note). A plist this check cannot parse at all has
        # no readable `WorkingDirectory`, so there is no directory to hand
        # `_is_private_checkout` — confirmed directly: `plutil -extract`
        # fails identically to `plutil -lint` on a genuinely corrupt file,
        # so there is no partial read to resolve a crew from. Building a
        # SECOND privacy resolution for "which crew does this filename
        # belong to" would be worse than the exposure it closes (steward,
        # 2026-08-21) — a plist filename can itself name a crew
        # (`com.convivy.cfo-review.plist` genuinely contains "cfo"), so the
        # shared signal reports only a COUNT; the actual filenames go to this
        # process's own local stderr log only, same as a private checkout's
        # finding already does.
        findings += 1
        names = ", ".join(unparseable)
        title = f"[serving-checkout] {len(unparseable)} plist(s) could not be read"
        body = (
            f"**This check could not parse {len(unparseable)} plist(s) under "
            f"`{launch_agents}` at all**, so it has no idea whether the "
            f"service(s) they define are serving from a stale checkout.\n\n"
            f"An unreadable plist here is reported rather than silently "
            f"skipped on purpose — a hand survey of exactly this question "
            f"(run with a stricter plist parser) missed 16 of 29 real "
            f"launchd-referenced checkouts this same way and reported the "
            f"scope as \"13, so it's small\" instead of \"16 unreadable\".\n\n"
            f"Which plists: check this run's own log — the names are not "
            f"repeated here, because a plist's own filename can itself name "
            f"a crew this check must not disclose, and it cannot resolve "
            f"privacy for a file it cannot parse in the first place."
        )
        # THE COUNT, NOT A HASH OF THE NAMES (security-reviewer, PR #424
        # round 1, blocking — a plain digest was the wrong fix, not just an
        # incomplete one). Launchd plist filenames in this fleet are drawn
        # from a small, largely-public namespace
        # (`com.convivy.<crew>[-suffix].plist`) — a reader of the shared
        # store can enumerate a few dozen plausible names, hash each one,
        # and match against a published digest in microseconds. SHA-256 is
        # one-way against a large or unknown input space; it is not private
        # against a small, guessable one, which is exactly what a plist
        # filename is here. Fixing that properly would need an HMAC keyed
        # with a secret that never leaves this machine — real
        # infrastructure this script has no other reason to hold, for a
        # narrow benefit (exact-set dedup precision). Coarser dedup buys
        # the same practical property (this run's finding does not spam a
        # fresh signal every 15 minutes while the SAME NUMBER of plists stay
        # broken) from a value with no connection to which plists they are.
        #
        # AND THE DATE, NOT JUST THE COUNT. The store's dedup_key is unique for
        # all time (store.py insert_signal looks it up with no time filter), so a
        # bare count-based key would log a same-count condition once, ever, and a
        # later, different incident with that count would vanish into it. Scoping
        # the key to today's date bounds that to one signal per day while a count
        # persists (not 96/day at this job's 15-minute interval), and a later day
        # logs again. Matches `convivy-orient/market_digest.py`'s
        # `market-digest:<ISO-week>`, an established shape for bounded periodic
        # dedup in this codebase.
        #
        # WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT COVER, WITHIN ONE DAY. A same-count condition that
        # recurs later the same day has a byte-identical dedup_key, so the store
        # answers it `duplicate` and nothing new is logged until the count changes
        # or the date rolls over. The `[UNPARSEABLE]` stderr line below still logs
        # every run, so this is not a total blind spot.
        res = _post_signal("agent-platform", title, body,
                           dedup_key=f"serving-checkout:unparseable:{_today()}:count-{len(unparseable)}",
                           severity="medium", dry_run=opts.no_report)
        print(f"[UNPARSEABLE] {names} → {res.get('status', '?')}", file=sys.stderr)
    elif opts.verbose:
        print("[OK] every plist under launch_agents was readable")

    # The pass reached the end without the host ever refusing to fork — the
    # outage (if there was one) is over, so the next one starts counting from
    # zero rather than inheriting this run's unrelated streak. This is ALSO
    # the only point that records which serving checkouts this pass actually
    # examined — a skipped pass has no examination of its own to record
    # (security-reviewer, PR #871 round 2) — so, unlike the skip counter
    # alone in round 1, every completed pass writes this, not only the ones
    # that are also resetting a nonzero streak.
    #
    # NEVER A PRIVATE CHECKOUT'S NAME (security-reviewer, PR #871 round 3).
    # `_is_private_checkout` gates this list exactly as it already gates
    # every per-repository finding below — a black-box crew's checkout name
    # and plist labels must not reach `FORK_SKIP_STATE_PATH` on disk any more
    # than they may reach `_post_signal`, since `_skip_pass_for_fork_exhaustion`
    # reads this list straight back and renders it, unfiltered, into a
    # finding body bound for the shared store. Filtered here, once, at the
    # one point this list is ever written — not at render time — so the
    # state file on disk never holds what the shared store must never see.
    checked = [f"{repo_dir.name} ({', '.join(checkouts[repo_dir])})"
              for repo_dir in sorted(checkouts) if not _is_private_checkout(repo_dir)]
    _write_state(consecutive_skips=0, checked=checked, checked_at=_utc_now_iso())

    for item in pending:
        repo_dir, services, f, private = (
            item["repo_dir"], item["services"], item["finding"], item["private"])
        findings += 1
        if private:
            # Local log only — never signal_client, never the shared
            # signal store. A black-box crew "files no issues and logs no
            # signals" (platform/decisions/0043); this is the equivalent
            # for a signal this script would otherwise log. The title/body
            # this finding would have used are never even built —
            # nothing here constructs text meant for a shared store.
            print(f"[FINDING] {repo_dir.name} (private crew, not logged): "
                  f"{f['reason']}", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        title = f"[serving-checkout] {repo_dir.name} {f['reason']}"
        paths_block = (
            "\n\nFiles changed from the committed state, outside `.claude/`:\n"
            + "\n".join(f"  - {p}" for p in f["paths"])
        ) if f["paths"] else ""
        body = (
            f"**`{repo_dir}` {f['reason']}**, and this checkout is what "
            f"the following launchd service(s) serve from directly:\n\n"
            f"{services}\n\n"
            f"Whatever this checkout sits on right now is what those services "
            f"are running — not what merged, not what `main` says, what is "
            f"literally on disk here.{paths_block}\n\n"
            f"Fix:\n\n"
            f"```\ncd {repo_dir}\ngit status\ngit switch main && git pull --ff-only\n```\n\n"
            f"If there is work in progress here, move it to a worktree first — "
            f"a live-serving checkout is not a place for feature work.\n\n"
            f"This check reads git metadata only (branch name, changed paths) "
            f"and never file contents."
        )
        # Each finding kind gets its own dedup_key — branch state and
        # dirty-tree state start and resolve independently, and a checkout
        # can be both off `main` AND dirty at once, so one key covering
        # "something about this checkout is wrong" would let one signal
        # silently swallow the other.
        res = _post_signal(repo_dir.name, title, body,
                           dedup_key=f"serving-checkout:{repo_dir.name}:{f['kind']}:{day}",
                           dry_run=opts.no_report)
        print(f"[FINDING] {repo_dir.name} ({services}): {f['reason']} → "
              f"{res.get('status', '?')}", file=sys.stderr)

    if findings == 0:
        if opts.verbose:
            print(f"[OK] every serving checkout ({len(checkouts)}) is on main and clean")
        return 0
    print(f"[DONE] {findings} finding(s) across {len(checkouts)} serving checkout(s)",
          file=sys.stderr)
    if _report_failures > 0:
        print(f"[REPORT-FAIL] {_report_failures} report(s) failed — "
              "findings not all logged", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
